Q5 – Race Conditions in a Cinema Seat-Booking System
Marks: 6
Bloom’s Level: K4 – Analyse
1. Problem Analysis
A cinema booking application allows many customers to book seats at the same time.

For example, 100 users try to book seats in a cinema containing only 10 seats.

The application must ensure:
- A seat can be booked by only one customer.
- Two users cannot book the same seat.
- The number of bookings cannot exceed the available seats.
- The booking operation must be thread-safe.
A race condition occurs when multiple threads access and modify shared data at the same time, producing an incorrect result.
2. Complete TODO 1 – Synchronized Method
The given code has an incomplete book() method and an incomplete thread loop. Use this complete program.

public class SeatBooking {
    private final boolean[] booked;
    private int bookingsMade = 0;

    SeatBooking(int seats) {
        booked = new boolean[seats];
    }

    // TODO 1: Make the booking operation atomic
    public synchronized void book(int seat) {
        if (!booked[seat]) {
            booked[seat] = true;
            bookingsMade++;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args)
            throws InterruptedException {

        SeatBooking screen = new SeatBooking(10);

        Thread[] users = new Thread[100];

        for (int i = 0; i < users.length; i++) {
            final int seat = i % 10;

            users[i] = new Thread(() -> screen.book(seat));
            users[i].start();
        }

        for (Thread t : users) {
            t.join();
        }

        System.out.println(
            "Seats sold: " + screen.bookingsMade);
    }
}



Expected output
Seats sold: 10


Explanation:
- There are 10 available seats.
- There are 100 users.
- Every 10 users compete for the same seat.
- The synchronized keyword allows only one thread at a time to execute the booking method on the same screen object.
- Once a seat is booked, other threads cannot book it again.
3. Version 1 – Without Synchronization
First, remove the synchronized keyword.
public void book(int seat) {
    if (!booked[seat]) {
        booked[seat] = true;
        bookingsMade++;
    }
}


Now multiple threads can execute the booking method simultaneously.

Important: The provided program does not guarantee that double booking will occur in every run. It may still print Seats sold: 10 even though the code is unsafe.

To make the race easier to reproduce, temporarily add a delay between checking and booking:
public void book(int seat) {
    if (!booked[seat]) {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(10);
        }
        catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            return;
        }

        booked[seat] = true;
        bookingsMade++;
    }
}


This delay is only for the experiment. It increases the opportunity for multiple threads to pass the check before either marks the seat as booked.
Example of an incorrect output
Seats sold: 47


This is an illustrative possible result, not a measured result. The actual output depends on the computer and thread scheduling.
4. How Does the Race Condition Occur? – Student A
Suppose two users, Thread A and Thread B, attempt to book seat 1.
Step	Thread A	Thread B
1	Checks seat 1	
2	Finds seat free	
3		Checks seat 1
4		Finds seat free
5	Marks seat booked	
6		Marks seat booked
7	Increases booking count	
8		Increases booking count

Both threads believe they successfully booked the same seat.

This is called a race condition.
Exact critical section
The following three operations must be treated as one atomic operation:
if (!booked[seat]) {
    booked[seat] = true;
    bookingsMade++;
}


The critical section includes:
1. Checking whether the seat is available.
2. Changing the seat status.
3. Increasing the booking count.
All three operations must be protected together.
