Why can the unsynchronized version sell more than 10 seats?

Multiple threads can read booked[seat] as false before any of them changes it to true. Each thread then increments bookingsMade. Therefore, the counter can become greater than the actual number of available seats.
6. Version 2 – Synchronized Block
Instead of synchronizing the entire method, we can synchronize only the critical section.
public void book(int seat) {
    synchronized (this) {
        if (!booked[seat]) {
            booked[seat] = true;
            bookingsMade++;
        }
    }
}


The synchronized(this) block uses the current SeatBooking object as its lock.

Only one thread can execute this block at a time for that object.
Expected output
Seats sold: 10


7. Comparison of Synchronization Strategies – Student B
Feature	Synchronized method	Synchronized block
Lock scope	Entire method	Only selected code
Lock object	Current object (this)	Specified lock object
Performance	May hold the lock longer	Can reduce lock duration
Complexity	Simple to write	More control over locking
Safety	Thread-safe	Thread-safe when the complete critical section is protected

Which is better?

For this small booking method, both approaches are correct and their performance difference may be very small. A synchronized block is useful when the method contains other operations that do not need locking.
8. Intrinsic Lock
Java provides an intrinsic lock, also called a monitor lock, for every object.
- A synchronized instance method locks the current object (this).
- A synchronized block locks the object specified inside its parentheses.
- Only one thread can hold a particular object's monitor at a time.
- Other threads attempting to acquire that same lock must wait.
For example:
public synchronized void book(int seat) {
    // Critical section
}


This is equivalent in locking behaviour to:
public void book(int seat) {
    synchronized (this) {
        // Critical section
    }
}


9. Static Synchronization
A static synchronized method locks the Class object rather than an individual instance.

Example:
public static synchronized void bookSeat() {
    // Shared class-level operation
}


For a cinema with multiple screens:
- Each screen can have its own SeatBooking object.
- Synchronizing an instance method locks only that screen's object.
- Different screens can process bookings concurrently.
- Static synchronization would be useful if all screens needed to protect one shared class-level resource.
Important: Using a static synchronized method for every screen's independent seat array would unnecessarily restrict concurrency across the entire cinema.
