11. Alternative Concept – Other Thread-Safe Solutions
The synchronized method can be replaced with other Java concurrency tools.
A. AtomicBoolean[] using compareAndSet()
AtomicBoolean provides an atomic operation that changes a seat from free to booked only if it is still free.
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

class AtomicBooking {
    AtomicBoolean[] seats;
    AtomicInteger bookingsMade = new AtomicInteger(0);

    AtomicBooking(int count) {
        seats = new AtomicBoolean[count];

        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
            seats[i] = new AtomicBoolean(false);
        }
    }

    void book(int seat) {
        if (seats[seat].compareAndSet(false, true)) {
            bookingsMade.incrementAndGet();
        }
    }
}


Advantages:
- Prevents two users from booking the same seat.
- Does not require a synchronized method.
- Different seats can be booked concurrently.
B. ReentrantLock
A ReentrantLock provides explicit control over locking and unlocking.
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

class LockBooking {
    boolean[] booked = new boolean[10];
    int bookingsMade = 0;

    ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    void book(int seat) {
        lock.lock();

        try {
            if (!booked[seat]) {
                booked[seat] = true;
                bookingsMade++;
            }
        }
        finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}


Advantages:
- Provides explicit lock and unlock operations.
- Supports features such as timed lock attempts and interruptible locking.
- finally ensures the lock is released.
C. ConcurrentHashMap using putIfAbsent()
putIfAbsent() atomically inserts a seat into the map only if it is not already present.
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

class MapBooking {
    ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, Boolean> booked =
        new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    AtomicInteger bookingsMade = new AtomicInteger(0);

    void book(int seat) {
        if (booked.putIfAbsent(seat, true) == null) {
            bookingsMade.incrementAndGet();
        }
    }
}


Advantages:
- Prevents duplicate seat bookings.
- Useful when seat information is stored using keys.
- Allows concurrent operations on different map entries.
12. Time and Space Complexity Analysis
Let s be the total number of seats.
Approach	Time per booking	Extra space
Synchronized method	O(1) work, plus possible lock waiting	O(1) lock overhead
Synchronized block	O(1) work, plus possible lock waiting	O(1) lock overhead
AtomicBoolean array	O(1) expected CAS operation; retries may occur under contention	O(s)
ReentrantLock	O(1) work, plus possible lock waiting	O(1) lock overhead
ConcurrentHashMap	O(1) expected insertion	O(s)

Time analysis under contention
- Without synchronization, multiple threads may access the same seat simultaneously, causing incorrect results.
- With synchronized methods or blocks, threads wait for the lock.
- With AtomicBoolean, multiple threads can attempt to book the same seat, but only one successful compare-and-set operation is allowed.
- With ReentrantLock, waiting threads must acquire the lock before booking.
- With ConcurrentHashMap, different seat keys can be processed concurrently.
For one booking, the basic check and update are O(1). However, actual completion time under heavy contention depends on thread scheduling and waiting.
Space analysis
For s seats:
- A boolean array uses O(s) space.
- An AtomicBoolean[] also uses O(s) space, with additional object overhead.
- A ConcurrentHashMap uses O(s) space when all seats are stored.
- A single lock adds O(1) lock storage per screen.
13. Joint Engineering Decision – Cinema With 20 Screens
Recommended approach
 Use a separate booking object and lock for each screen.

Reasons:
1. Each screen has independent seats.
2. Customers can book different screens simultaneously.
3. A lock on one screen does not block bookings on another screen.
4. The synchronized method is simple and easy to maintain.
5. It guarantees that the same seat cannot be booked twice.
For a high-traffic cinema, AtomicBoolean[] with compareAndSet() is another good choice because different seats can be booked concurrently within the same screen.

For a real cinema application with multiple application servers, Java synchronization alone is insufficient. The booking must also be protected by a database transaction or an atomic database update to prevent duplicate bookings across servers.
